          "question": "When a reference note names a root like K21, what do I look for to get to the specific code?",
          "answer": "To identify specific codes from a root like K21 in the ICD-10 classification system, you want to look for the following:\n\n1. **Root Meaning**: Understand that K21 relates to \"Gastro-esophageal reflux disease\" (GERD).\n2. **Subcodes**: Look for additional digits that follow the root code. The ICD-10 codes often consist of a letter followed by two digits (the root) and may have one or more additional digits that provide further specificity.\n3. **Specificity**: The more digits included in the code, the more specific the diagnosis or condition described. For K21, the full code can be K21.0 (Gastro-esophageal reflux disease with esophagitis) or K21.9 (Gastro-esophageal reflux disease without esophagitis).\n4. **Cross-Referencing**: It's useful to reference an official coding manual or database to see specific conditions listed under each root code.\n\nFor more detailed instructions or examples, consult the ICD-10 manual or a clinical coding guide. This will have the complete listing of what each specific code under the root indicates.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
